🚽 Test case: issue #271 -- `smol % 0` had no zero-divisor guard at all
🚽 and crashed the interpreter with SIGFPE.
🚽
🚽 handle_binary_operation()'s OP_MOD branch guarded VAR_INT, and OP_DIVIDE
🚽 guarded both VAR_INT and VAR_SHORT -- but OP_MOD's VAR_SHORT arm went
🚽 straight to the modulo. So `smol / 0` reported "Division by zero"
🚽 cleanly while `smol % 0`, one operator away, took down the process.
🚽
🚽 Both the int and short forms are exercised, so the fixture pins that
🚽 they AGREE -- same diagnostic, same printed value. That agreement is
🚽 the property that was missing, and one guard existing without the
🚽 other is exactly the shape of the bug.
🚽
🚽 ── What printing the result does NOT prove ───────────────────────────
🚽 An earlier version of this comment claimed the printed value would
🚽 catch an implementation that emitted the diagnostic but never wrote
🚽 the fallback. It would not, and the claim was removed rather than left
🚽 to be believed (PR #310 review).
🚽
🚽 `result` comes from SAFE_MALLOC (handle_binary_operation(), ast.c).
🚽 safe_malloc() is plain malloc behind a header and does not zero -- but
🚽 safe_free() memsets every block before releasing it, so a recycled
🚽 block comes back zeroed, and a page fresh from the OS is zeroed too.
🚽 An uninitialized read therefore yields the same 0 this fixture
🚽 expects, and deleting `*(short *)result = 0;` from the guard leaves
🚽 both fixtures green. Verified by mutation, not assumed.
🚽
🚽 So what is pinned here is the diagnostic, the agreement between the
🚽 two widths, and the absence of a crash -- which is the whole of #271.
🚽 Making the value distinguishable would mean choosing a fallback that
🚽 uninitialized memory does not reliably read as, and for a "there is no
🚽 answer" case that is not an improvement over 0.
skibidi main {
    rizz ia = 7;
    rizz ib = 0;
    yapping("rizz: %d", ia % ib);

    smol sa = 7;
    smol sb = 0;
    yapping("smol: %d", sa % sb);
    bussin 0;
}
